Booting the Computer

When you press the power button, the BIOS (Basic Input/Output System) or UEFI (Unified Extensible Firmware Interface) microchip is activated.
These chips contain booting instructions that load a special program called the bootloader, which then starts the operating system.
User Interaction with the OS

When you use an application, it sends your request to the operating system.
The OS interprets this request and directs it to the appropriate hardware component, which then sends information back to the OS and subsequently to the application.
Example: Using a Calculator

Clicking on the calculator application and typing numbers sends a request to the OS.
The OS sends the calculation to the CPU (Central Processing Unit), which performs the work and sends the answer back to the OS to be displayed in the application.
